            "Every society that has a system of law must answer a question that sounds simple but has divided philosophers for centuries: when someone commits a crime, what is the purpose of punishing them? The answer matters a great deal, because it shapes not only how long people spend in prison, but what happens to them while they are there, and what kind of people they are when they leave.",
            "The oldest answer is retribution. On this view, punishment is justified because the offender deserves it. A wrong has been done, and justice requires that the wrongdoer suffer in proportion to that wrong. Retribution is often confused with revenge, but its defenders insist on an important difference: revenge is personal and unlimited, whereas retribution is imposed by an impartial authority and must be proportionate. A minor theft cannot justify the same punishment as violence.",
            "A second answer looks forward rather than backward. Deterrence holds that punishment is justified by its effects: it discourages the offender from repeating the crime, and it discourages others from committing it in the first place. The logic is intuitive. Were it not for the fear of punishment, the argument goes, many more people would break the law. Yet the evidence is more complicated than the logic. Research in many countries suggests that the certainty of being caught deters crime far more effectively than the severity of the punishment. Doubling a prison sentence has little effect if offenders do not expect to be caught at all.",
            "The third answer is incapacitation: while someone is in prison, they cannot harm the public. For a small number of dangerous offenders, this is a powerful argument. For the great majority of prisoners, however, who will eventually be released, incapacitation simply delays the question of what happens next.",
            "This brings us to the fourth answer, rehabilitation, which aims to change the offender so that they no longer wish, or need, to commit crimes. Rehabilitation programmes include education, vocational training, treatment for addiction and psychological support. Critics have sometimes dismissed this approach as soft, or as rewarding criminals with opportunities that law-abiding citizens do not receive. Supporters reply that it is the most practical answer of all, since nearly every prisoner will one day become someone's neighbour.",
            "The evidence on rehabilitation is encouraging, though not uniformly so. Several studies have found that prisoners who take part in education programmes are significantly less likely to reoffend after release, and that the cost of such programmes is far smaller than the cost of returning people to prison. Some Scandinavian countries, which place rehabilitation at the centre of their prison systems, report reoffending rates well below those of many other countries. Critics point out, reasonably, that these societies differ in many other ways too, so the prison system alone cannot explain the difference.",
            "In recent decades, a fifth approach has gained attention: restorative justice. Rather than focusing only on the offender and the state, it brings victims into the process. In carefully prepared meetings, offenders hear directly how their actions affected other people and take responsibility for repairing the harm, whether through apology, compensation or community work. Victims who take part often report greater satisfaction than those whose cases go only through the courts. Restorative justice is not suitable for every crime or every victim, and it must never be forced on anyone, but where it is used carefully, it appears to reduce reoffending as well.",
            "In practice, of course, no legal system relies on a single theory. Most combine elements of all of them: a sentence must be proportionate, it should deter, it may protect the public, and it ought to give the offender a realistic chance to change. The real debate is about balance. A system that thinks only of retribution risks becoming cruel and ineffective; one that thinks only of rehabilitation risks losing the public's trust that wrongdoing will be taken seriously.",
            "Perhaps the most useful test of any approach is a simple one. It is essential that we ask not only whether a punishment feels deserved, but whether the society that imposes it is safer as a result. On that test, the evidence suggests that the most effective systems are those that make punishment certain, proportionate and purposeful, and that never forget that the person being punished will one day walk out of the prison door.",
